course: dictionary
lesson: 2484
title: DDT and the food chain
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: DDT is an abbreviation, a short form of a very long chemical name, dichlorodiphenyltrichloroethane. Writers shorten long names to save space, and a dictionary gives you the full name. DDT is uncountable, so we say «some DDT», not «a DDT».
explain: DDT is a chemical that was sprayed to kill insects. Context helps here: «The farmers sprayed DDT on their crops to kill pests.» The words sprayed, crops and pests tell you DDT is a poison for insects.
explain: Look for clue words like harm, banned and protected, because they show what happened next. «The pesticide DDT made the eagles' eggshells so thin that they cracked.» Thin eggshells show DDT harmed birds too, not only insects. Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence and seeing if it still makes sense.
fact: DDT is short for dichlorodiphenyltrichloroethane.
fact: DDT is a strong chemical insecticide, which means it kills insects.
fact: DDT was found to harm wildlife, especially birds of prey such as eagles and ospreys, whose eggshells became thin and broke.
fact: Chemicals like DDT can build up along a food chain, from tiny water insects to fish to the birds that eat the fish.
fact: DDT was banned for farm use in many countries, but small amounts are still used against malaria mosquitoes in some places.
exercise: «The farmers sprayed DDT on their crops to kill pests.» What is DDT? | A farmer's tractor | *A strong chemical that kills insects | A kind of bird that lives near the sea | A type of crop grown on farms
exercise: DDT is short for a very long chemical name. What is a short form like this called? | A food chain | *An abbreviation | A synonym | An insecticide
exercise: «The eagles' eggshells were so thin that they cracked.» What made the eggshells thin? | The parents sat on them too gently | *DDT, which harmed the birds | The eggs were too big to fit in the nest | The weather was too cold
test: In a food chain, which animals were most harmed by DDT? | Plants | *Birds of prey, such as eagles and ospreys | Tiny water bugs | Fish
test: Why is DDT still used in some places? | It is sprayed on crops to make them taste sweet | *It is used in small amounts against malaria mosquitoes | It is a type of food for fish | It makes eggshells thicker
exam: Ben reads «DDT» in a science book and wants to know its full name. What should he do? | Ask his teacher to say it is a plant | *Look it up in a dictionary, because DDT is an abbreviation | Guess that it is a type of bird | Skip it, because it never matters
